/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Visible light runs from about 400 nm (violet) to 700 nm (red); ultraviolet (UV) is the band below 400 nm.
   - Let I₀ be the light intensity entering the solution and I the intensity leaving it.
   - T is transmittance (no unit); I₀ is the incident light intensity; I is the transmitted light intensity; A is absorbance (no unit).
   - Absorbance is the useful quantity because it is linear in concentration.
   - The law states that absorbance is proportional to concentration and to the light path. ε is molar absorptivity, a property of the substance at a stated wavelength and temperature.
   - A is absorbance (no unit); ε is molar absorptivity in L mol⁻¹ cm⁻¹; c is concentration in mol/L; l is the light path through the solution in cm.
   - A blank cuvette sets the reference.
   - For routine work, prepare standards of known concentration, read their absorbances, and plot A against c.
   - A single-beam spectrophotometer has six parts in a row.
   - A colorimeter, also called a filter photometer, is the same chain with a coloured glass or interference filter in place of the monochromator.
*/
