/* book exam traps: Forgetting the blank: reagent colour and cuvette absorption then add to the sample and give a falsely high absorbance. | Mixing up absorbance and transmittance: A is a logarithm, so doubling the concentration doubles A but squares T. From A = 0.10 to 0.20, T goes from 0.79 to 0.63, not to 0.40; from A = 0.30 to 0.60, T goes from 0.50 to 0.25. Both relations hold only inside the  | Using a glass cuvette for UV work: glass absorbs UV strongly below about 340 nm, so very little light reaches the detector and the reading becomes noisy and distorted by stray light. If the sample cell is glass while the blank is quartz, the extra absorption i | Assuming the calibration stays straight at high absorbance: above about A = 1.5, stray light bends the line; dilute the sample and repeat. | Changing the light path: A = ε c l, so a 0.5 cm cuvette halves A; standards and samples must use the same path length. */
