/* book: lead: A flame photometer measures sodium, potassium, lithium, calcium or barium by the light their atoms emit in a hot flame. A diluted sample is sprayed into the flame. Atoms absorb heat, become excited, and fall back emitting light at fixed wavelengths. A filter selects one wavelength and a photodetector measures the brightness. The method exists because these elements are hard to measure by simple co */
