/* book exam traps: Forgetting the dilution factor: 2.5 mmol/L is the diluted concentration; the serum value is 125 mmol/L in the worked example. | Calling the measurement absorption, or applying the Beer–Lambert law directly to the emitted flame light. A flame photometer measures emission. | Using the wrong filter: sodium is read at 589 nm, potassium at 766 nm, and lithium at 671 nm. | Calibrating only at high concentration, where self-absorption bends the curve downward and makes the sample look lower than it is. | Ignoring the internal standard and flame drift, then reporting a false potassium result; the potassium channel also reads a ratio to lithium. */
